{"abstract":"Strassen’s asymptotic rank conjecture [Progr. Math. 120 (1994)] claims a strong submultiplicative upper bound on the rank of a three-tensor obtained as an iterated Kronecker product of a constant-size base tensor. The conjecture, if true, most notably would put square matrix multiplication in quadratic time. We note here that some more-or-less unexpected algorithmic results in the area of exponential-time algorithms would also follow. Speci cally, we study the so-called set cover conjecture, which states that for any ϵ > 0 there exists a positive integer constant k such that no algorithm solves the k-Set Cover problem in worst-case time O((2 −ϵ)n|F |poly(n)). The k-Set Cover problem asks, given as input an n-element universeU, a family F of size-at-most-k subsets of U, and a positive integer t, whether there is a subfamily of at most t sets in F whose union isU. The conjecture was formulated by Cygan, Fomin, Kowalik, Lokshtanov, Marx, Pilipczuk, Pilipczuk, and Saurabh in the monograph Parameterized Algorithms [Springer, 2015], but was implicit as a hypothesis already in Cygan, Dell, Lokshtanov, Marx, Nederlof, Okamoto, Paturi, Saurabh, and Wahlström [CCC 2012, ACM Trans. Algorithms 2016], there conjectured to follow from the Strong Exponential Time Hypothesis. Weprove that if the asymptotic rank conjecture is true, then the set cover conjecture is false. Using a reduction by Krauthgamer and Trabelsi [STACS 2019], in this scenario we would also get an O((2 −δ)n)-time randomized algorithm for some constant δ > 0 for another well-studied problem for which no such algorithm is known, namely that of deciding whether a given n-vertex directed graph has a Hamiltonian cycle. At a ne-grained level, our results do not need the full strength of the asymptotic rank conjecture; it su ces that the conclusion of the conjecture holds approximately for a single 7 × 7 × 7 tensor.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Björklund, Andreas","Kaski, Petteri"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-789550"],"filters":{"added":"2025-02","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Finland"],"glorg":["Aalto University","All Global Affiliations"],"keyword":["Randomized algorithm","Set partitioning","asymptotic rank conjecture"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the asymptotic rank conjecture and the set cover conjecture are not both true","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3618260.3649656","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-789550","identifier":[{"id":"85196674696","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-4776ffe4-4b0f-41b1-a2a4-94ca5e086bcf"],"keyword":["asymptotic rank conjecture","directed Hamiltonian cycle","randomized algorithm","set cover conjecture","set partitioning","three-way partitioning"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-B55CBD460BDE21E34B1CBBE725D04005"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3618260.3649656","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Theoretical Computer Science, Algorithms"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"fi","key":"aalto university","loc":["Finland","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Aalto University","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Andreas","key":"björklundpau","last":"Björklund","locid":"7e6e312c-7d1b-4d6a-ac22-385bab06ee41","name":"Björklund, Andreas","orcid":"0009-0009-9303-9986","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Petteri","key":"kaskipau","last":"Kaski","name":"Kaski, Petteri","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3618260.3649656","status":"p","title":"STOC 2024: Proceedings of the 56th Annual ACM Symposium on Theory of Computing"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85196674696"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-4776ffe4-4b0f-41b1-a2a4-94ca5e086bcf"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"The Asymptotic Rank Conjecture and the Set Cover Conjecture Are Not Both True","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-789550","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Finding a Hamiltonian cycle in a given graph is computationally challenging, and in general remains so even when one is further given one Hamiltonian cycle in the graph and asked to find another. In fact, no significantly faster algorithms are known for finding another Hamiltonian cycle than for f inding a first one even in the setting where another Hamiltonian cycle is structurally guaranteed to exist, such as for odd-degree graphs. We identify a graph class– the bipartite Pfaffian graphs of minimum degree three– where it is NP-complete to decide whether a given graph in the class is Hamiltonian, but when presented with a Hamiltonian cycle as part of the input, another Hamiltonian cycle can be found efficiently. We prove that Thomason’s lollipop method [Ann. Discrete Math., 1978], a well-known algorithm T for finding another Hamiltonian cycle, runs in a linear number of steps in cubic bipartite Pfaffian A graphs. This was conjectured for cubic bipartite planar graphs by Haddadan [MSc thesis, Waterloo, 2015]; in contrast, examples are known of both cubic bipartite graphs and cubic planar graphs E where the lollipop method takes exponential time. Beyond the reach of the lollipop method, we address a slightly more general graph class and present two algorithms, one running in linear-time and one operating in logarithmic space, that take as input (i) a bipartite Pfaffian graph G of minimum degree three, (ii) a Hamiltonian cycle H in G, and (iii) an edge e in H, and output at least three other Hamiltonian cycles through the edge e in G. We also present further improved algorithms for finding optimal traveling salesperson tours and counting Hamiltonian cycles in bipartite planar graphs with running times that are not achieved yet in general planar graphs. Our technique also has purely graph-theoretical consequences; for example, we show that every cubic bipartite Pfaffian graph has either zero or at least six distinct Hamiltonian cycles; the latter case is tight for the cube graph.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Björklund, Andreas","Kaski, Petteri","Nederlof, Jesper"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-789557"],"filters":{"added":"2025-02","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Finland","Netherlands"],"glorg":["Aalto University","All Global Affiliations","Utrecht University"],"keyword":["Planar graph"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"another hamiltonian cycle in bipartite pfaffian graphs","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.4230/LIPICS.ICALP.2024.26","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["Andreas Björklund: Supported by the VILLUM Foundation, Grant 16582. Jesper Nederlof: Supported by the project CRACKNP that has received funding from the European Research Council (ERC) under the European Union’s Horizon 2020 Research and Innovation Programme, Grant 853234."],"id":"S01-000.00-789557","identifier":[{"id":"85198383918","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-73721b26-7a63-4696-aea5-2265fafd57e2"],"keyword":["Another Hamiltonian cycle","Pfaffian graph","Thomason’s lollipop method","planar graph"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-18FF7F80C6DB1E4CEC1E75441E895273"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC0","type":"rem","url":"https://dblp.org/rec/conf/icalp/BjorklundKN24","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.4230/LIPICS.ICALP.2024.26","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Theoretical Computer Science, Algorithms"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"fi","key":"aalto university","loc":["Finland","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Aalto University","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"nl","key":"utrecht university","loc":["Netherlands","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Utrecht University","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Andreas","key":"björklundpau","last":"Björklund","locid":"7e6e312c-7d1b-4d6a-ac22-385bab06ee41","name":"Björklund, Andreas","orcid":"0009-0009-9303-9986","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Petteri","key":"kaskipau","last":"Kaski","name":"Kaski, Petteri","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Jesper","key":"nederlofpau","last":"Nederlof","name":"Nederlof, Jesper","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-73721b26-7a63-4696-aea5-2265fafd57e2"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Another Hamiltonian Cycle in Bipartite Pfaffian Graphs.","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-789557","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"PURPOSE: Clinical relapse is the major threat for patients with myelodysplastic syndrome (MDS) undergoing hematopoietic stem-cell transplantation (HSCT). Early detection of measurable residual disease (MRD) would enable preemptive treatment and potentially reduced relapse risk. METHODS: Patients with MDS planned for HSCT were enrolled in a prospective, observational study evaluating the association between MRD and clinical outcome. We collected bone marrow (BM) and peripheral blood samples until relapse, death, or end of study 24 months after HSCT. Patient-specific mutations were identified with targeted next-generation sequencing (NGS) panel and traced using droplet digital polymerase chain reaction (ddPCR). RESULTS: Of 266 included patients, estimated relapse-free survival (RFS) and overall survival (OS) rates 3 years after HSCT were 59% and 64%, respectively. MRD results were available for 221 patients. Relapse was preceded by positive BM MRD in 42/44 relapses with complete MRD data, by a median of 71 (23-283) days. Of 137 patients in continuous complete remission, 93 were consistently MRD-negative, 39 reverted from MRD+ to MRD-, and five were MRD+ at last sampling. Estimated 1 year-RFS after first positive MRD was 49%, 39%, and 30%, using cutoff levels of 0.1%, 0.3%, and 0.5%, respectively. In a multivariate Cox model, MRD (hazard ratio [HR], 7.99), WHO subgroup AML (HR, 4.87), TP53 multi-hit (HR, 2.38), NRAS (HR, 3.55), and acute GVHD grade III-IV (HR, 4.13) were associated with shorter RFS. MRD+ was also independently associated with shorter OS (HR, 2.65). In a subgroup analysis of 100 MRD+ patients, presence of chronic GVHD was associated with longer RFS (HR, 0.32). CONCLUSION: Assessment of individualized MRD using NGS + ddPCR is feasible and can be used for early detection of relapse. Positive MRD is associated with shorter RFS and OS (ClinicalTrials.gov identifier: NCT02872662).","author_count":33,"contributors":["Tobiasson, Magnus","Pandzic, Tatjana","Illman, Johanna","Nilsson, Lars","Weström, Simone","Ejerblad, Elisabeth","Olesen, Gitte","Björklund, Andreas","Olsnes Kittang, Astrid","Werlenius, Olle","Lorentz, Fryderyk","Rasmussen, Bengt","Cammenga, Jörg","Weber, Duruta","Lindholm, Carolin","Wiggh, Joel","Dimitriou, Marios","Moen, Ann Elin","Yip Lundström, Laimei","von Bahr, Lena","Baltzer-Sollander, Karin","Jädersten, Martin","Kytölä, Soili","Walldin, Gunilla","Ljungman, Per","Groenbaek, Kirsten","Mielke, Stephan","Jacobsen, Sten Eirik W.","Ebeling, Freja","Cavelier, Lucia","Smidstrup Friis, Lone","Dybedal, Ingunn","Hellström-Lindberg, Eva"],"cuid":["S02-093.08-735200"],"filters":{"added":"2024-08","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Tobiasson, Magnus"],"country":["Finland","Norway","Sweden"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Haukeland University Hospital","Helsinki University Central Hospital","Helsinki University Hospital","Karolinska Institute","Karolinska Institutet","Lund University","Oslo University Hospital","Sahlgrenska University Hospital","Umeå University","University Hospital of Umeå","University of Bergen","University of Gothenburg","University of Oslo","Uppsala University","Örebro University"],"keyword":["Graft vs Host Disease etiology","Hematopoietic Stem Cell Transplantation methods","Humans","Leukemia Myeloid Acute genetics","Neoplasm Recurrence Local genetics","Prognosis","Prospective Studies","Recurrence"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-SDR"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-SDR","DI-U-AU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"patient specific measurable residual disease markers predict outcome in patients with myelodysplastic syndrome and related diseases after hematopoietic stem cell transplantation","src":["au","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85189800901","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S02-093.08-735200","identifier":[{"id":"85189800901","type":"scopus"},{"id":"38232336","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["au-6bc405ed-a539-4f84-a193-4a8cf93c8445","sdu-3b3974f3-2bbf-432d-9107-ee4097b2c669"],"keyword":["Graft vs Host Disease/etiology","Hematopoietic Stem Cell Transplantation/methods","Humans","Leukemia, Myeloid, Acute/genetics","Myelodysplastic Syndromes/therapy","Neoplasm Recurrence, Local/genetics","Neoplasm, Residual/genetics","Prognosis","Prospective Studies","Recurrence"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-5DC8364692ADC6291AE5E765D363A300"],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"se","key":"karolinska institutet","loc":["Sweden","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Karolinska Institutet","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"se","key":"karolinska institute","loc":["Sweden","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Karolinska Institute","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"se","key":"uppsala university","loc":["Sweden","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Uppsala University","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"fi","key":"helsinki university central hospital","loc":["Finland","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Helsinki University Central Hospital","used":1},{"affno":"5","country":"fi","key":"helsinki university hospital","loc":["Finland","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Helsinki University Hospital","used":1},{"affno":"6","country":"se","key":"lund university","loc":["Sweden","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Lund University","used":1},{"affno":"7","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"